## 5. Data Loading — COPY INTO vs External Tables vs Iceberg

### COPY INTO (internal tables) — what we used
Data is physically copied into Snowflake's internal columnar storage.

```sql
COPY INTO raw_flights FROM @gcs_bronze_stage
FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = 'JSON' STRIP_OUTER_ARRAY = TRUE);
```

**Pros:** Fastest queries, full Snowflake feature support, automatic optimization
**Cons:** Storage cost in Snowflake + original storage (duplication), loading time for large datasets

### External tables
Data stays in GCS/S3. Snowflake queries it in place like Trino/Presto.

```sql
CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE raw_flights_ext
    LOCATION = @gcs_bronze_stage
    FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = 'JSON');
```

**Pros:** No storage duplication, always fresh (no loading needed)
**Cons:** Slower queries, limited Snowflake features (no Dynamic Tables, limited optimization)

### Iceberg tables (newest approach)
Snowflake reads Apache Iceberg format directly from your data lake.

```sql
CREATE ICEBERG TABLE flights
    EXTERNAL_VOLUME = 'my_gcs_volume'
    CATALOG = 'SNOWFLAKE'
    BASE_LOCATION = 'bronze/routes/';
```

**Pros:** No copy, full Snowflake features, works with Spark/Flink/Trino too
**Cons:** Requires Iceberg format, more setup

### When to use which
| Scenario | Approach |
|---|---|
| Small dataset, max query speed | COPY INTO |
| Large historical archive | External table |
| Active data lake with Iceberg | Iceberg table |
| Hot/cold tiering | COPY INTO recent + External for historical |

