## 7. Storage Integrations — Cross-Cloud Authentication

A storage integration lets Snowflake authenticate with external cloud storage without storing credentials.

### How it works (what we did)
1. Create integration in Snowflake → Snowflake generates a service account
2. Grant that service account read access to your GCS bucket
3. Snowflake uses the service account to authenticate with GCS

```sql
CREATE STORAGE INTEGRATION gcs_skypulse_integration
    TYPE = EXTERNAL_STAGE
    STORAGE_PROVIDER = 'GCS'
    ENABLED = TRUE
    STORAGE_ALLOWED_LOCATIONS = ('gcs://skypulse-triptide/');

DESC INTEGRATION gcs_skypulse_integration;
-- Returns STORAGE_GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT — grant this objectViewer on your bucket
```

**Key insight:** Snowflake maintains Google service accounts even when running on AWS. This enables cross-cloud data access without VPNs or network configuration — purely IAM-based.

### For AWS S3
Instead of a service account, Snowflake returns an IAM user ARN and external ID. You create a trust relationship in AWS IAM.

