## 9. Dynamic Tables — Automated Transformations

Dynamic Tables are materialized views that automatically refresh when source data changes. No scheduler, no DAG, no manual refresh.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE DYNAMIC TABLE hourly_route_summary
    TARGET_LAG = '1 hour'
    WAREHOUSE = SKYPULSE_WH
AS
SELECT
    route_id, origin, destination, date, season, is_weekend,
    AVG(avg_price_usd) as avg_price,
    COUNT(DISTINCT airline_name) as airline_count
FROM SKYPULSE.GOLD.flight_price_summary
GROUP BY route_id, origin, destination, date, season, is_weekend;
```

### TARGET_LAG
How stale the Dynamic Table can be before Snowflake refreshes it. Options:
- `'1 hour'` — refresh at most once per hour
- `'5 minutes'` — near real-time
- `'1 day'` — daily refresh
- `DOWNSTREAM` — refresh only when queried

### Dynamic Table chains
Multiple Dynamic Tables can form a chain. When the source changes, Snowflake propagates the refresh through the chain automatically:

```
raw_flights → flight_price_summary (DT) → hourly_route_summary (DT)
```

When `raw_flights` gets new rows via Snowpipe, both downstream Dynamic Tables refresh according to their TARGET_LAG.

### Dynamic Tables vs Regular Views vs Materialized Views
| | Regular View | Materialized View | Dynamic Table |
|---|---|---|---|
| Data stored | No (computed on query) | Yes | Yes |
| Refresh | Every query | Manual/scheduled | Automatic |
| Supports aggregations | Yes | Limited | Yes |
| Supports joins | Yes | Limited | Yes |
| Cost | Warehouse per query | Storage + refresh | Storage + refresh |
| Best for | Simple transformations | Pre-computed aggregates | Complex pipelines |

