## 12. Snowpark — Python Inside Snowflake

Snowpark lets you write Python (or Java/Scala) code that runs inside Snowflake's compute engine. The code is pushed down to Snowflake — your data never leaves.

### Snowpark DataFrame API
Similar to PySpark DataFrames but running on Snowflake:

```python
from snowflake.snowpark import Session

session = Session.builder.configs(connection_params).create()

df = session.table("SKYPULSE.SILVER.raw_flights")
df_filtered = df.filter(df["departure_iata"] == "ORD")
df_aggregated = df_filtered.group_by("arrival_iata").agg(
    F.count("*").alias("flight_count"),
    F.avg("avg_price_usd").alias("avg_price")
)
df_aggregated.write.save_as_table("SKYPULSE.GOLD.route_summary")
```

### Snowpark vs PySpark
| | PySpark | Snowpark |
|---|---|---|
| Runs on | Spark cluster (EMR, Dataproc) | Snowflake warehouse |
| Data location | HDFS, S3, GCS | Snowflake internal storage |
| Language | Python, Scala, Java, R | Python, Java, Scala |
| When to use | Large-scale ETL, ML pipelines | Snowflake-native transforms |

### Snowpark User-Defined Functions (UDFs)
```python
@udf
def classify_route(destination: str) -> str:
    intl = ['LHR','CDG','NRT','PVG','AMS']
    return 'International' if destination in intl else 'Domestic'

session.call_udf('classify_route', df['arrival_iata'])
```

